Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (Acute Musculoskeletal Complication)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Generalized joint hypermobility, Skin hyperextensibility, Atrophic papyraceous scarring

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-020
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, recurrent joint dislocation causing immobilizing pain in a patient with an established connective tissue disorder.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with known Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome who suffered an acute, painful joint subluxation/dislocation event that they were unable to self-reduce. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate for neurovascular compromise distal to the injured joint, to facilitate safe and timely manual reduction, and to provide adequate analgesia before daytime clinical services opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My kneecap popped completely out of place when I stood up, and I can't push it back in this time because the pain is too sharp."
History of Present Illness: Patient with established hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome presents with an acute right lateral patellar dislocation. Timeline: Patient has a history of recurrent joint subluxations following minimal mechanical stress. Ten hours ago, while rising from a low chair, the patient felt the right knee give way, followed by immediate, severe, localized pain. The patient noted the patella was laterally displaced. Previous self-reduction techniques failed due to severe secondary muscle spasm. Patient reports chronic, widespread musculoskeletal pain that is currently poorly responsive to their baseline standard analgesia.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized on the living room floor; completely unable to weight-bear or flex the right knee.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to safely mobilize to access food, water, or hygiene facilities.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, in visible acute distress, holding the right knee strictly extended.
Physical Exam: Generalized joint hypermobility confirmed visually (capable of Beighton score maneuvers). Skin hyperextensibility noted on the volar forearm (>1.5 cm stretch). Papyraceous, widened atrophic scarring visible over the elbows. Right knee exam reveals obvious lateral displacement of the patella with a pronounced loss of the normal anterior anatomical contour. The knee is locked in extension. Exquisite tenderness over the medial retinaculum. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are strong and symmetric. Sensation intact globally in the lower limb. No signs of compartment syndrome.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting on a rug; safe space available for manual reduction maneuvers.
Medication Reconciliation: Baseline Paracetamol and Meloxicam taken today; entirely inadequate for the acute dislocation pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Lateral Patellar Dislocation secondary to underlying Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Quadriceps Tendon Rupture - Ruled out clinically by the obvious lateral physical displacement of the patella, rather than a superiorly retracted muscle mass with an empty patellar pouch.

Tibial Plateau Fracture - Ruled out by the clear mechanism of atraumatic subluxation in a hypermobile patient, and the visible anatomical deformity of the patella itself rather than strictly the joint line.

Acute Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out by soft, compressible calf compartments, strong distal pulses, and absence of pain out of proportion to the visible anatomical dislocation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient, highly vulnerable due to severe underlying connective tissue laxity, presented with an acute, unreduced patellar dislocation resulting in total functional immobility and severe pain. Delaying clinical assessment until the next in-hours period was medically inappropriate due to the risk of progressive chondral damage, severe unmanaged pain, and the unacceptable functional state of being stranded on the floor. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the specific nature of the dislocation, excluded limb-threatening vascular compromise, and established a safe environment to administer analgesia and perform a gentle, controlled manual reduction, immediately restoring basic anatomical alignment and mitigating severe distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Entonox (Nitrous Oxide/Oxygen) via patient-controlled mask for procedural analgesia. Successfully performed a gentle closed reduction of the right patella via slow extension of the knee with medial pressure. Prescribed a 3-day course of Codeine 30mg PRN for acute post-reduction pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Keep the knee strictly locked in the provided rigid extension splint for the next 48 hours during any necessary movement. Consequence: Prevention of sudden flexion that would allow the hypermobile patella to slip out again, providing a sense of physical security and reducing fear (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply ice wrapped in a towel to the inner side of the kneecap for 20 minutes every 2 hours. Consequence: Immediate constriction of the torn internal ligaments, drastically reducing the swelling and throbbing pain (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral back to the patient's primary physiotherapist for stabilization exercises.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a rigid knee immobilizer splint. Arranged standard crutches.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to organize a follow-up ultrasound or MRI to assess medial patellofemoral ligament integrity.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via ambulance if the foot becomes cold, pale, or completely numb, or if the calf develops severe, tight, localized swelling.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of the dislocation is clinical and successfully reduced. Long-term management of EDS requires multidisciplinary specialist care. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The patella is back in place, but the supporting ligaments are severely stretched or torn. You will experience significant aching and swelling over the next week. Strict immobilization is required initially.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you lose feeling in your toes, if your foot turns cold or blue, or if you develop new severe pain in your calf muscle.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because of your EDS, your ligaments are extremely stretchy, allowing the kneecap to slide completely off its track. The gas and air relaxed the muscles enough for me to gently push it back. The rigid splint acts as an artificial external ligament to hold everything in place while the acute swelling settles.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to bend the knee. Use the crutches to offload the weight. Ice the inside of the knee rigorously to halt the internal bleeding from the stretched tissues.

